.TH MIN 1 "2026" "nimo" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
min \- make your workspace clean
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B min
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nimo
makes a Mac feel minimal again: a desktop with nothing on it, a dock with only
the apps you actually open, a calm wallpaper, sane preferences, and a disk
without gigabytes of cache on it.
.PP
Every command that changes something shows what it is about to do and waits for
a yes. Every change nimo makes can be reversed with
.BR "min undo" .
nimo never touches a path outside your home folder and never asks for sudo.
.SH THE PROMPT
Run
.B min
with no command and it opens a prompt. Type
.B /clean desktop
,
.B /wallpaper
,
.B /undo
\- the slash is optional, as is the exact wording. Typos are matched on shared
prefix and suggested, never run on your behalf. Flags work there too. Piped or
redirected,
.B min
prints this help instead.
.PP
.B /auto
stops the asking for the rest of the session. It explains what that covers and
asks once; the prompt then reads \fBnimo auto \(rs\(rs>\fR until you leave or
type \fB/auto off\fR. \fBNIMO_AUTO=1\fR in the environment starts there.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B min clear \fR[\fIwhat\fR]
Ask what should be clear \- desktop, downloads, screenshots, dock, disk \- and
do that one. Naming it outright, as \fBmin clear desktop\fR, skips the asking.
.TP
.B min tidy
Desktop, dock, wallpaper and preferences, in that order. Each step asks first.
.TP
.B min desktop
Move everything on the desktop into \fIDesktop/Tidy/\fR, sorted by kind.
Nothing is deleted or renamed. \fB\-\-undo\fR puts it all back.
.TP
.B min downloads
The same sorter, pointed at \fI~/Downloads\fR.
.TP
.B min screenshots
Gather every loose screenshot into \fI~/Pictures/Screenshots\fR.
.TP
.B min zen
Stop drawing icons on the desktop. Nothing moves; \fB\-\-restore\fR brings
them back.
.TP
.B min dark \fR/\fB min light
Switch the appearance. The previous setting is written down first.
.TP
.B min hide
Let the menu bar slide away \- on Windows, the taskbar.
\fB\-\-restore\fR brings it back. This is the only command in nimo that
restarts Explorer, because the taskbar reads the setting when Explorer starts
and at no other time; it asks separately before doing so, and nothing is lost
by it.
.TP
.B min duplicates
Report files kept in more than one place. Never deletes anything.
.TP
.B min startup
List everything that launches when you sign in \- the registry's Run key and
the Startup folder on Windows, the login items on macOS \- and stop the ones
you tick. Nothing is uninstalled and nothing is deleted: a program you stop
still starts when you open it yourself. Entries set for everyone on the
machine need an administrator, so nimo lists those and leaves them alone.
\fB\-\-undo\fR puts back what it moved.
.TP
.B min apps
List apps not opened in six months, biggest first. On Windows there is no
record of when a program was last opened, so it lists what has been installed
longest instead, and says so. Never uninstalls anything.
.TP
.B min blank \fIFILE\fR
Empty a file, keeping a copy of what was in it in \fI~/.nimo/undo\fR, so
\fBmin undo\fR can put the contents back. The file itself stays where it is.
.TP
.B min dock \-\-fast
No wait before the dock slides, and a quick slide. Both settings are written
down first, so \fBmin setup \-\-restore\fR puts the sluggish ones back.
.TP
.B min empty
Empty the Trash, or the Recycle Bin on Windows. The one command with no undo.
nimo never reaches into the Recycle Bin itself \- it asks Windows what is in
there and asks Windows to empty it.
.TP
.B min dock
List the dock and remove only the entries you tick. The dock is exported first;
\fB\-\-restore\fR brings it back. On Windows it does the same for the apps
pinned to the taskbar: a pin is a shortcut plus a blob under Taskband that
Explorer keeps in step with it, so nimo moves the shortcut aside and clears
the blob together, and \fB\-\-restore\fR puts both back. Nothing is
uninstalled either way.
.TP
.B min wallpaper
Choose from the wallpapers your system already ships with \- macOS's Desktop
Pictures, or the folders under \fIC:\(rs\(rsWindows\(rs\(rsWeb\fR. Nothing is downloaded.
\fB\-\-random\fR picks for you, \fB\-\-restore\fR goes back.
.TP
.B min undo
Everything nimo has done, listed. Pick one and it is reversed.
.TP
.B min clean
Scan for junk, show the total, and free it after you confirm.
.TP
.B min preview
The same scan, unable to delete anything.
.TP
.B min top
The biggest folders in your home, with bars.
.TP
.B min doctor
Disk, memory, battery, uptime, clutter and total junk on one screen.
.TP
.B min setup
Apply a small, sane set of preferences \- \fBdefaults\fR on macOS, HKCU registry
values on Windows, never anything needing an administrator. Every previous value and
type are written down first, so \fB\-\-restore\fR can put them back exactly,
including unsetting keys that had never been set.
.TP
.B min version
Print the version and stop.
.TP
.B min update
Run \fBgit pull\fR in whichever folder nimo lives in.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would happen and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation question.
.TP
.B \-\-undo
Put the desktop back the way it was.
.TP
.B \-\-restore
Put the dock, the wallpaper or the preferences back.
.TP
.B \-\-random
Let nimo pick the wallpaper.
.TP
.BI \-\-only " a,b"
Limit to certain items or setup groups.
.TP
.BI \-\-days " N"
What counts as an old download. Default 30.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
How many rows to show. Default 15.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine readable output for \fBclean\fR and \fBpreview\fR. Sizes in bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List what a command would offer, without asking anything.
.TP
.B \-\-menu
Pick from a list with the arrow keys. Where the arrows cannot work \- a pipe,
a script, \fBTERM=dumb\fR \- the same list is numbered and typed instead.
.TP
.B \-\-fast
With \fBmin dock\fR: no wait before the dock slides, and a quicker slide.
.TP
.BI \-\-by " WHAT"
Sort into folders by \fBkind\fR (the default), \fBletter\fR or \fBdate\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-auto
Yes to everything, in one command and in the prompt. The flag spelling of
\fB/auto\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-color
Colour even when the output is piped.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Plain output. \fBNO_COLOR\fR in the environment does the same.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-animation
Do not draw the face in, and do not blink.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
No banner.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.nimo/config
Optional defaults, one \fIkey = value\fR per line: \fBdays\fR, \fBlimit\fR,
\fBjobs\fR, \fBcolor\fR, \fBtidy\fR. It is read, never executed.
.TP
.I ~/.nimo/undo/
One file per desktop tidy, listing every move nimo made.
.TP
.I ~/.nimo/backups/
The dock as it was, and the wallpaper as it was.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything went fine, including "there was nothing to do".
.TP
.B 1
Something could not be done.
.TP
.B 2
The command line did not make sense.
.SH TESTS
.B bash test/run.sh
runs the whole suite against a throwaway computer in \fI/tmp\fR.
.B bash test/macos.sh
runs more against a simulated macOS and a simulated Windows, so the dock,
wallpaper, registry and preference
code can be tested without a Mac and without touching yours.
.SH SEE ALSO
The full documentation lives in the repository README.
.SH AUTHOR
Daniel Kopa.
